¶
'''upblaze''' (up-blāz′), ''v. i.'' To blaze up; shoot up, as a flame. ''Southey'', Thalaba, vi. 8.

'''upblow†''' (up-blō′), ''v.'' '''I.''' ''trans.'' To blow up; inflate.
{{smaller block|His belly was ''upblowne'' with luxury.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. iv. 21.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To blow up from a given quarter or point.
{{smaller block|<poem>The watry Southwinde, from the seabord coste
''Upblowing.'' {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. iv. 13.}}</poem>}}

'''upbraid''' (up-brād′), ''v.'' [< ME. ''upbraiden'', ''upbrayden'', ''upbreiden'', ''upbreyden'', ''oupbreiden'', reproach, lit. ‘seize upon, attack’; < ''up'' + ''braid''<sup>1</sup>, scold: see ''braid''<sup>1</sup> and ''abraid''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To reproach for some fault or offense; charge reproachfully; reproach: regularly followed by ''with'' or ''for'' (rarely ''of'') before the thing imputed.
{{smaller block|<poem>If you refuse your aid, . . . yet do not
''Upbraid'' us ''with'' our distress.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., v. 1. 35.}}
It were a thing monstrously absurd and contradictory to give the parliament a legislative power, and then to ''upbraid'' them ''for'' transgressing old establishments.
{{right|''Milton'', Ans. to Eikon Basilike, § 19. (''Richardson.'')}}}}
'''2†.''' To offer as an accusation or charge against some person or thing: with ''to'' before the person or thing blamed.
{{smaller block|You shall be very good friends hereafter, and this never to be remembered or ''upbraided.''
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Epicœne, iv. 2.}}
<poem>It hath been ''upbraided to'' men of my trade
That oftentimes we are the cause of this crime.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Bartholomew Fair, iii. 1.}}
<poem>May they not justly ''to'' our Climes ''upbraid''
Shortness of Night? {{right|''Prior'', Solomon, i.}}</poem>}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>A knight, whom from childhode
He had ''updrawe'' into manhode.</poem>
{{right|''Gower'', Conf. Amant., v. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}

¶
'''updress†''' (up-dres′), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''updressen''; < ''up'' + ''dress.''] To set up; prepare.
{{smaller block|<poem>He wolde ''updresse''
Engyns, bothe more and lesse,
To cast at us, by every side.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 7067.}}}}

'''upend''' (up-end′), ''v. t.'' To set on end, as a barrel.
{{smaller block|An approaching heavy sea may carry the boat away on its front, and turn it broadside on, or ''up-end'' it.
{{right|''Luce'', Seamanship, p. 611.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Upending-tongs''', heavy tongs with a swinging support, used in iron-works to turn the bloom, that the hammer may strike upon its end.}}

'''upfill''' (up-fil′), ''v. t.'' To fill up; make full. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|A cup . . . to the brim ''upfild.'' {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. iii. 42.}}}}
